-- title:
--   Positive dilations preserve a double cone
-- statement:
--   Work in $d$-dimensional Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$ (Lean: `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`, with the
--   dimension $d$ implicit). For a unit vector $v$ and an angle $\vartheta$, write
--   $V(v,\vartheta)$ for the **double cone** `doubleCone v ϑ`: the set of non-zero points whose direction makes
--   an angle **strictly less than** $\vartheta$ with the axis $v$ or with $-v$, i.e.
--   $$V(v,\vartheta) \;=\; \tilde V(v,\vartheta) \cup \bigl(-\tilde V(v,\vartheta)\bigr), \qquad \tilde V(v,\vartheta) = \{\,h \ne 0 : \langle v,h\rangle/\|h\| > \cos\vartheta\,\},$$
--   the open solid cone of half-aperture $\vartheta$ about $v$ together with its reflection through the origin; the origin itself belongs to neither.
--
--   Let $v \in \mathbb{R}^d$ satisfy $\|v\| = 1$, let $\vartheta$ be an angle with
--   $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, let $t > 0$ be a real scalar, and let $p \in V(v,\vartheta)$. Then
--
--   $$t\,p \;\in\; V(v,\vartheta).$$
--
--   The lemma records the scale invariance of double cones: a cone is determined by directions alone,
--   so membership is unaffected by stretching a point along the ray through it. It is the elementary
--   step used whenever cone membership has to be transported between the integer lattice
--   $\mathbb{Z}^d$ and a rescaled lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypotheses $\|v\| = 1$ and $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$ are the standing
--   normalisations under which the double cone is defined (a unit axis and an aperture in the range
--   where the angle condition is meaningful); they are carried here even though the geometric content
--   is only the positivity of the scalar $t$.
